"In Lesson 2, villages stored grain. In Lesson 3, people in Uruk wrote down who owed what. Uruk was more than a village. It was a city, with temples, workshops, and at least 25,000 people, many of whom did not farm at all. A city needs a lot of extra food, more than its farmers eat. Where could that much food come from? In most of the places where the first cities grew, the answer was a river. Southern Mesopotamia gets almost no rain, but the Euphrates and Tigris bring water from the mountains. Egypt is mostly desert, but every summer the Nile rose and left fresh mud on its fields. Rivers gave water for crops, soil that stayed rich, fish and reeds, and a road for boats. Heavy loads, from grain to stone, traveled far more easily by water than over land. Yet the rivers were not alike. The Nile's flood came at about the same time every year, and Egyptian farmers could plan around it. In Mesopotamia the high water came at the wrong time for the crops, so people had to lead water to the fields with canals and dikes. The rivers of the Indus Valley, fed by the summer monsoon, changed over the centuries as their floods and the monsoon rains grew weaker. Along the Yellow River in China, great floods could break through and change the river's course. Each river shaped its city in its own way. Many textbooks speak of four great river civilizations: Mesopotamia, Egypt, the Indus, and China's Yellow River. The list is a useful start, but it leaves a lot out. Liangzhu, beside the Yangtze, built dams and a walled city more than 5,000 years ago. Caral, in Peru, grew in a desert valley beside the small Supe River at about the same time as the first Egyptian pyramids. Cities began in more places than four, and none of them is the “first” in every way. Cities could organize huge projects. In Egypt, the Great Pyramid of Khufu rose more than 4,500 years ago, originally about 146 meters tall. It was not built by slaves. Archaeologists have found the town that housed the builders of the Giza pyramids, with bakeries and storerooms. A Harvard project describes the workers as Egyptians paid in food, mainly bread and beer; some researchers think many came in rotating teams from their home districts. Boats carried its fine limestone down the Nile. How the blocks were raised to the top is still not fully known. Living beside a river had risks. Floods could destroy fields, and a river that changed its path could leave a city without water. The Indus cities declined slowly after about 1900 BCE, and changing river flows are one of the main explanations. A river did not make a city by itself. People did, by learning how their river behaved and working together to manage it.": "e26d00814448",
